","url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey","html_url":"https://github.com/maxcherey","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"\"gitlab\" is gitlab specific, I suggest to use \"external_id\" for this column","created_at":"2026-02-26T15:58:36Z","updated_at":"2026-02-26T15:58:36Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/3#discussion_r2859857229","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/3","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/2859857229"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/3#discussion_r2859857229"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/3"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/2859857229/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":16,"original_line":16,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":16,"position":16,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/2859863424","pull_request_review_id":3861932699,"id":2859863424,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s6qdg2A","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,136 @@\n+# Table: `git.repo`\n+\n+## Overview\n+\n+**Purpose**: Store GitLab repository metadata including project IDs, paths, namespace information, and activity timestamps.\n+\n+**Data Source**: GitLab API via custom git service\n+\n+---\n+\n+## Schema Definition\n+\n+| Column | Type | Constraints | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|-------------|\n+| `id` | integer | PRIMARY KEY, AUTO INCREMENT | Internal primary key |\n+| `gitlab_repo_id` | integer | UNIQUE, NOT NULL | GitLab project ID |\n+| `path` | varchar(500) | NOT NULL | Repository path (e.g., group/project) |","path":"streams/raw_git/git_repo.md","commit_id":"7b4811e24e84830df73aabdea58e332784a65b32","original_commit_id":"81dc8a7b1b188af1a8973df750823b68fb578879","user":{"login":"maxcherey","id":191410199,"node_id":"U_kgDOC2iwFw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/191410199?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey","html_url":"https://github.com/maxcherey","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/maxcherey/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"\"path\" is confusing a little bit.\nI suggest to name it either \"project\" or \"organization\" or \"group\"","created_at":"2026-02-26T15:59:37Z","updated_at":"2026-02-26T15:59:37Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/3#discussion_r2859863424","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/3","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/2859863424"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/3#discussion_r2859863424"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/3"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/2859863424/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":17,"original_line":17,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":17,"position":17,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/2865939315","pull_request_review_id":3868770566,"id":2865939315,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDORRWv8s6q0sNz","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,297 @@\n+# Identity Resolution\n+\n+## Overview\n+\n+The Monitor platform collects user data from multiple independent sources (Git, Zulip, GitLab, Constructor, BambooHR, HubSpot, YouTrack). A single employee may have different usernames, emails, and accounts across these systems. The **identity resolution pipeline** unifies these scattered records into a single profile per person.\n+\n+## Core Concept\n+\n+Each source record is decomposed into **(token, rid)** pairs, where:\n+\n+- **token** — a value that can identify a person: username, email, work_email, etc. Each source may produce any number of tokens.\n+- **rid** — a deterministic identifier for the source account: `rid = cityHash64(source, source_id)`.\n+\n+Each pair `(token, rid)` forms a **segment endpoint**. Two records belong to the same person if they share a token — like two segments sharing an endpoint. The algorithm transitively groups all records connected through shared tokens (full value match, not partial).\n+\n+## Data Sources\n+\n+Each source normalizes raw data into `(source, source_id, token)` tuples. A single source record may produce multiple tokens:\n+\n+| Source | Token fields | Notes |\n+|---|---|---|\n+| Git | author, email | Lowercased |\n+| Zulip | full_name, email | |\n+| GitLab | username, email, commit_email, public_email | Multiple emails per user |\n+| Constructor | username, full_name, email | Handle + full name if different |\n+| BambooHR | first_name + last_name, work_email | Dots replaced with spaces in names |\n+| HubSpot | first_name + last_name, email | From users + owners tables |\n+| YouTrack | username, email | |\n+\n+## Walkthrough Example\n+\n+The following example traces one person — Alexei Vavilov — through the entire pipeline. He appears across three source systems under different usernames and emails.\n+\n+### Source Data\n+\n+**BambooHR:**\n+\n+| id | first_name | last_name | work_email |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| b1 | Alexei | Vavilov | Alexei.Vavilov@alemira.com |\n+\n+**Git commits:**\n+\n+| hash | author | email |\n+|---|---|---|\n+| c1 | he4et | he4ethb1u@gmail.com |\n+| c2 | he4et | he4et@oddsquat.org |\n+| c3 | he4et | a.vavilov@gmail.com |\n+\n+**YouTrack:**\n+\n+| id | username | email |\n+|---|---|---|\n+| y1 | Alexey Vavilov | a.vavilov@constructor.tech |\n+\n+### Step 1 — Unified References\n+\n+Each source record is decomposed into `(source, source_id, token, rid, meta)` tuples. `rid = cityHash64(source, source_id)` — all tokens from the same source account share the same `rid`. Each `(token, rid)` pair is a segment that the algorithm will use for grouping:\n+\n+| source | source_id | token | rid | meta |\n+|---|---|---|---|---|\n+| bamboo | b1 | alexei vavilov | h1 | |\n+| bamboo | b1 | alexei.vavilov@alemira.com | h1 | |\n+| git | c1 | he4et | h2 | |\n+| git | c1 | he4ethb1u@gmail.com | h2 | |\n+| git | c2 | he4et | h3 | |\n+| git | c2 | he4et@oddsquat.org | h3 | |\n+| git | c3 | he4et | h4 | |\n+| git | c3 | a.vavilov@gmail.com | h4 | |\n+| youtrack | y1 | alexey vavilov | h5 | |\n+| youtrack | y1 | a.vavilov@constructor.tech | h5 | |\n+\n+At this point the algorithm can already group some records. Running min-propagation on this data produces **3 groups**:\n+\n+| Group | Members | Connected by |\n+|---|---|---|\n+| 1 | h2, h3, h4 | shared token `he4et` |\n+| 2 | h1 | isolated — no shared tokens |\n+| 3 | h5 | isolated — no shared tokens |\n+\n+Bamboo (h1) and YouTrack (h5) remain separate because their tokens don't overlap with anything else. The enrichment steps below will bridge these gaps.\n+\n+### Step 2 — Manual Identity Pairs\n+\n+Injects **synthetic bridge records** from a seed table. Each pair forces two accounts into the same identity group by creating records with a shared `rid`.\n+\n+This step does not apply to our example, but a pair like:\n+\n+```\n+v.samun@examus.net  <->  vsamun@examus.net\n+```","path":"IDENTITY_RESOLUTION.md","commit_id":"7b4811e24e84830df73aabdea58e332784a65b32","original_commit_id":"7b4811e24e84830df73aabdea58e332784a65b32","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Add language identifiers to fenced code blocks.**\n\nBoth code blocks are missing language specifiers, which affects rendering and accessibility.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\nFor the identity pair example (lines 89-91):\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n v.samun@examus.net  <->  vsamun@examus.net\n ```\n```\n\nFor the transitive closure diagram (lines 188-195):\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n h2 ←— \"he4et\" —→ h3\n h2 ←— \"he4et\" —→ h4\n h4 ←— \"a.vavilov@gmail.com\" —→ h5\n h5 ←— \"alexey vavilov\" —→ h1\n\n Result: h1, h2, h3, h4, h5 → profile_group_id = 1\n ```\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 188-195\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧰 Tools</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🪛 markdownlint-cli2 (0.21.0)</summary>\n\n[warning] 89-89: Fenced code blocks should have a language specified\n\n(MD040, fenced-code-language)\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n````\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@IDENTITY_RESOLUTION.md` around lines 89 - 91, Add language identifiers to the\ntwo fenced code blocks: for the identity pair block containing\n\"v.samun@examus.net  <->  vsamun@examus.net\" and for the transitive closure\ndiagram block containing the lines with h2, h3, h4, h5, h1; update the opening\nbackticks for each to use ```text so the blocks render/accessibility metadata is\ncorrect.\n````\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-27T19:50:40Z","updated_at":"2026-02-27T19:50:40Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/3#discussion_r2865939315","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/3","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/2865939315"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/3#discussion_r2865939315"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/3"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/comments/2865939315/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":89,"original_start_line":89,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":91,"original_line":91,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"NONE","original_position":91,"position":91,"subject_type":"line"}],"source_reviews":[{"id":3840072782,"node_id":"PRR_kwDORRWv8s7k4txO","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 10**\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Due to the large number of review comments, Critical, Major severity comments were prioritized as inline comments.\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Duplicate comments (6)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_ms365/ms365_teams_activity.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`30-30`: **Same `lastActivityDate NOT NULL` inconsistency as `ms365_onedrive_activity.md`**\n\nThis is especially likely to surface for external/guest users (`isExternal = true`), who may have no activity in a given reporting period.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_ms365/ms365_teams_activity.md` at line 30, The schema declares\nthe column `lastActivityDate` as NOT NULL in ms365_teams_activity (same issue as\nms365_onedrive_activity) which will break for external/guest users with no\nactivity; update the `lastActivityDate` column definition to allow NULL (remove\nor change the NOT NULL constraint) in ms365_teams_activity.md and mirror the\nsame nullable change you applied in ms365_onedrive_activity.md so the\ndocumentation and any downstream schema generation accept absent activity dates\nfor `isExternal = true`.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`15-15`: **Same `unique` reserved keyword issue as `ms365_onedrive_activity.md`**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_ms365/ms365_teams_activity.md` at line 15, The schema uses the\nreserved word `unique` as a column name (see the column definition `unique |\ntext | PRIMARY KEY`) which conflicts with other files; rename the column to a\nnon-reserved identifier (e.g., `unique_id` or `activity_id`) and update all\nreferences to it in this file and any place that consumes this schema\n(parsers/ingestion code, queries, and related docs). Ensure the PRIMARY KEY\ndeclaration moves to the new name and run a quick search for `unique` within\nms365_teams_activity.md (and related stream handlers) to replace usages so the\nschema and code remain consistent.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_ms365/ms365_sharepoint_activity.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`20-20`: **Same `lastActivityDate NOT NULL` inconsistency as `ms365_onedrive_activity.md`**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_ms365/ms365_sharepoint_activity.md` at line 20, The table schema\nfor ms365_sharepoint_activity.md marks lastActivityDate as NOT NULL but mirrors\nthe same inconsistency flagged in ms365_onedrive_activity.md; update the column\ndefinition for lastActivityDate in ms365_sharepoint_activity.md to match the\nintended contract (either remove NOT NULL or make it nullable) so it aligns with\nthe onedrive doc and other stream schemas, and ensure the column description\nremains \"Last SharePoint activity date\".\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`15-15`: **Same `unique` reserved keyword issue as `ms365_onedrive_activity.md`**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_ms365/ms365_sharepoint_activity.md` at line 15, The schema uses\nthe reserved word `unique` as a column name/primary key which conflicts with SQL\nkeywords; rename the column to a non-reserved identifier (e.g., `unique_id` or\n`record_id`) everywhere it appears in this schema entry and update its\ntype/constraint (e.g., \"`unique_id` | text | PRIMARY KEY | Unique record\nidentifier\") to avoid keyword collisions and maintain consistency with the\nsimilar change made for ms365_onedrive_activity.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_ms365/ms365_email_activity.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`15-15`: **Same `unique` reserved keyword issue as `ms365_onedrive_activity.md`**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_ms365/ms365_email_activity.md` at line 15, The schema uses the\nreserved keyword `unique` as a column name/PRIMARY KEY; rename this field to a\nnon-reserved identifier (e.g., `unique_id` or `id`) throughout the stream\ndefinition in ms365_email_activity (and mirror the same change you made in\nms365_onedrive_activity) and update any references to `unique` (primary key\ndeclarations, mappings, and downstream references) so the schema and code no\nlonger use the reserved word `unique`.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`43-46`: **Same PII data classification gap as `ms365_onedrive_activity.md`**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_ms365/ms365_email_activity.md` around lines 43 - 46, The schema\ndoc entry for userPrincipalName lacks the same PII classification and handling\ndetails as the other stream (ms365_onedrive_activity); update the\n**userPrincipalName** field description to explicitly mark it as PII (matching\nthe classification used in ms365_onedrive_activity), include sensitivity level\n(e.g., \"PII — email address\"), required handling recommendations\n(masking/hashed-at-rest, limited access, logging/audit rules), and any\nretention/processing notes so the field's privacy treatment is consistent across\nMS365 stream docs.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🟡 Minor comments (17)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_zulip/zulip_users.md-62-62 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`62-62`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Missing moderator role (300) from the role hierarchy**\n\nThe Zulip API encodes roles as: Organization owner: 100 | Organization administrator: 200 | Organization moderator: 300 | Member: 400 | Guest: 600. The moderator tier is absent from both the Field Semantics (line 62) and the Notes section (line 123).\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-**Values**: Numeric role codes (e.g., 100 = owner, 200 = admin, 400 = member, 600 = guest)\n+**Values**: Numeric role codes (e.g., 100 = owner, 200 = admin, 300 = moderator, 400 = member, 600 = guest)\n```\n\n```diff\n-Zulip roles are numeric with lower values indicating higher permissions. Common values: 100 (owner), 200 (admin), 400 (member), 600 (guest).\n+Zulip roles are numeric with lower values indicating higher permissions. Common values: 100 (owner), 200 (admin), 300 (moderator), 400 (member), 600 (guest).\n```\n</details>\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 123-123\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_zulip/zulip_users.md` at line 62, The Roles documentation is\nmissing the Organization moderator (300); update the \"Values\" entry under Field\nSemantics to include \"300 = moderator\" alongside the existing codes and also add\na corresponding mention in the Notes section referencing the moderator role\n(Organization moderator: 300) so the role hierarchy lists owner (100), admin\n(200), moderator (300), member (400), guest (600); modify the \"Values\" line and\nthe Notes paragraph to explicitly include the 300 moderator code and brief\nlabel.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_zulip/zulip_messages.md-85-94 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`85-94`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**`DATE(created_at)` is session-timezone-sensitive**\n\n`created_at` is `timestamptz`. Calling `DATE(created_at)` without an explicit timezone converts using the session's `TimeZone` GUC, so the day boundary shifts depending on where the query runs. Use an explicit timezone to ensure deterministic bucketing.\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-    DATE(created_at) as day,\n-    SUM(count) as messages,\n+    DATE(created_at AT TIME ZONE 'UTC') as day,\n+    SUM(message_count) as messages,\n```\n\n```diff\n-GROUP BY DATE(created_at)\n+GROUP BY DATE(created_at AT TIME ZONE 'UTC')\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_zulip/zulip_messages.md` around lines 85 - 94, The query uses\nDATE(created_at) which is session-timezone-sensitive for the timestamptz column;\nconvert created_at to a fixed timezone (e.g., UTC) before extracting the date to\nmake bucketing deterministic — replace DATE(created_at) with an explicit\ntimezone-aware expression like (created_at AT TIME ZONE 'UTC')::date (or\nDATE(created_at AT TIME ZONE 'UTC')) in the SELECT, GROUP BY and ORDER BY so the\nsame deterministic day boundary is used everywhere when querying zulip_messages.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_cursor/cursor_events.md-15-29 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`15-29`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**`unique` is a SQL reserved keyword — add a schema-level quoting warning**\n\n`UNIQUE` is among the PostgreSQL/SQL92 reserved words that are not allowed as unquoted identifiers. The first SQL example correctly quotes it as `\"unique\"` (line 118), but there is no note in the schema section warning consumers that this column **must** be double-quoted in every query context. Without a schema-level callout, copy-paste into JOIN conditions or WHERE clauses will silently produce a syntax error.\n\nAdd a note under the `unique` row in the schema table, e.g.:\n\n> ⚠️ `unique` is a SQL reserved word — always reference it as `\"unique\"` (double-quoted) in queries.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_cursor/cursor_events.md` around lines 15 - 29, The schema uses\nthe column name unique which is a SQL reserved word; update the cursor_events\nschema table entry for the unique column to include a schema-level warning\ninstructing readers to always reference it as \"unique\" (double-quoted) in\nqueries, and mention that the first SQL example already uses \"unique\" (see the\nexisting quoted usage) so consumers must double-quote it in JOINs, WHERE\nclauses, and any SQL contexts to avoid syntax errors.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_cursor/cursor_daily_usage.md-70-73 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`70-73`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**`day` field format is unspecified**\n\nThe semantics section describes `day` only as \"Text representation of the day\" with no example or format. Given that it's a standalone `text` column with its own `NOT NULL` constraint, consumers need to know the exact format (e.g., `\"2026-01-15\"`, `\"Mon Jan 15 2026\"`, `\"Monday\"`) to use it correctly in display or grouping. Add at least one concrete `Example` value, as done for `date`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_cursor/cursor_daily_usage.md` around lines 70 - 73, The doc for\nthe `day` column lacks a concrete format example; update the `day` field\ndocumentation for the `day` column to specify the exact textual format consumers\nshould expect (e.g., ISO date string like \"2026-01-15\" or weekday name such as\n\"Monday\"), add an explicit Example line mirroring the `date` field style, and\nclarify whether it is a full date, weekday name, or human-readable label so\ncallers using `day` for display/grouping (referencing the `day` field) can parse\nand group consistently.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_cursor/cursor_events_token_usage.md-139-143 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`139-143`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Cost formula semantics and NULL-safety are undocumented**\n\nTwo gaps in the cost-calculation note:\n\n1. **Pre vs. post-discount ambiguity**: It's unclear whether `totalCents` already reflects a discount or is the gross amount. If it's already discounted, applying `discountPercentOff` again double-discounts. Clarify which interpretation is correct.\n2. **NULL not handled**: When `discountPercentOff` is `NULL` (a valid state per the schema), the formula evaluates to `NULL`. Add a `COALESCE(discountPercentOff, 0)` note so consumers don't silently get `NULL` effective costs.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_cursor/cursor_events_token_usage.md` around lines 139 - 143,\nClarify that totalCents refers to the gross amount before any discounts (so the\ndiscountPercentOff must be applied once to compute effective_cost), and update\nthe documentation for the effective_cost formula to use NULL-safe semantics by\nwrapping discountPercentOff with COALESCE(discountPercentOff, 0) — i.e., state\nthe formula as effective_cost = totalCents * (1 - COALESCE(discountPercentOff,\n0) / 100) and explicitly note that if discountPercentOff is NULL it is treated\nas 0 (no discount).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_cursor/cursor_daily_usage.md-221-234 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`221-234`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**`ORDER BY` in the feature usage breakdown excludes `bugbot`**\n\nLine 233 sorts by `chat + cmdk + composer + agent DESC`, but `bugbot` is selected and semantically part of total feature usage. Users with high `bugbotUsages` but low other counts will be ordered incorrectly. Either include it in the expression or document the intentional omission.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🐛 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-ORDER BY chat + cmdk + composer + agent DESC;\n+ORDER BY chat + cmdk + composer + agent + bugbot DESC;\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_cursor/cursor_daily_usage.md` around lines 221 - 234, The ORDER\nBY expression in the cursor_daily_usage query omits bugbot usage, causing\nincorrect ranking for users with high bugbotUsages; update the ORDER BY clause\nto include SUM(bugbotUsages) (or the alias bugbot) so it sorts by the true total\n(e.g., chat + cmdk + composer + agent + bugbot) or add a clarifying comment if\nomission is intentional; locate this in the SQL block selecting email, SUM(...)\nas chat/cmdk/composer/agent/bugbot from cursor_daily_usage and adjust the ORDER\nBY accordingly.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_cursor/cursor_events_token_usage.md-115-125 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`115-125`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**`SUM(t.inputTokens + t.outputTokens)` silently undercounts when either field is NULL**\n\nSince both `inputTokens` and `outputTokens` are `NULLABLE`, any row where one is `NULL` makes the entire addition `NULL`, and that row contributes nothing to the `SUM`. Use `COALESCE` to treat missing values as zero:\n\n<details>\n<summary>🐛 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-    SUM(t.inputTokens + t.outputTokens) as total_tokens\n+    SUM(COALESCE(t.inputTokens, 0) + COALESCE(t.outputTokens, 0)) as total_tokens\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_cursor/cursor_events_token_usage.md` around lines 115 - 125, The\nSUM expression SUM(t.inputTokens + t.outputTokens) can return NULL for rows\nwhere either inputTokens or outputTokens is NULL; update the aggregation to use\nCOALESCE so missing token counts are treated as zero (e.g., replace\nSUM(t.inputTokens + t.outputTokens) with SUM(COALESCE(t.inputTokens, 0) +\nCOALESCE(t.outputTokens, 0))) in the query that references\ncursor_events_token_usage (alias t) and cursor_events (alias e) so all rows\ncontribute correctly to total_tokens.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_cursor/cursor_daily_usage.md-207-218 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`207-218`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Use `::timestamptz` instead of `::timestamp` to avoid epoch conversion TZ ambiguity**\n\nLine 214 casts the literal as `::timestamp` (no timezone), so `EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM ...)` interprets it in the database server's local timezone. If the server is not UTC this produces an incorrect epoch boundary. Since the `date` field stores UTC-based epoch milliseconds, cast to `timestamptz`:\n\n<details>\n<summary>🐛 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-WHERE date >= EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM '2026-01-01'::timestamp) * 1000\n+WHERE date >= EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM '2026-01-01'::timestamptz) * 1000\n```\n</details>\n\nThe same fix applies to the analogous filter in the \"AI contribution to code\" and \"Feature usage breakdown\" examples.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_cursor/cursor_daily_usage.md` around lines 207 - 218, The WHERE\nclause in the SQL example uses EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM '2026-01-01'::timestamp) which\nis processed in the server's local TZ and can shift the intended UTC epoch;\nchange the cast to ::timestamptz (i.e., '2026-01-01'::timestamptz) in the\ncursor_daily_usage query (the literal inside the WHERE clause that computes\nepoch milliseconds) and make the identical change for the same literal/filter in\nthe \"AI contribution to code\" and \"Feature usage breakdown\" examples so all\nepoch boundary comparisons use timestamptz.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/stream_communication/communication_events.md-57-65 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`57-65`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**`source_id` Format description only documents the MS365 pattern.**\n\nThe field's **Format** entry states `{source}:{unique_id}:{channel}` as the general format, but Zulip's `source_id` is `{source}:{unique_id}` (no channel component). The Notes section correctly documents both variants, but the primary Format field should reflect both to avoid confusion for new consumers reading only the field semantics.\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Suggested documentation fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-- **Format**: Composite key as `{source}:{unique_id}:{channel}` to ensure uniqueness across sources and channels\n+- **Format**: `{source}:{unique_id}:{channel}` for multi-channel sources (MS365), or `{source}:{unique_id}` for single-channel sources (Zulip)\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 373-374\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/stream_communication/communication_events.md` around lines 57 - 65,\nThe Format line for the source_id field currently only shows the full\n`{source}:{unique_id}:{channel}` pattern and should be expanded to document both\nvariants used by different sources (MS365 uses `{source}:{unique_id}:{channel}`,\nZulip uses `{source}:{unique_id}`); update the Format entry for `source_id` to\neither show both explicit examples or use an optional-channel notation like\n`{source}:{unique_id}[:{channel}]`, and apply the same change to the other\noccurrence mentioned (lines referencing the same field around 373-374) so\nreaders of `source_id` see both variants at a glance.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_git/git_commit.md-16-16 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`16-16`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Widen `hash` column to `varchar(64)` to support SHA-256 hashes.**\n\nSHA-256 produces a 64-character hexadecimal hash (vs. SHA-1's 40), and `varchar(40)` will truncate or reject SHA-256 hashes. GitLab supports SHA-256 repositories via Gitaly (currently experimental, feature-flagged at project creation), and Git allows per-repository adoption via `git init --object-format=sha256`. Update to `varchar(64)` (or `char(64)`) to future-proof this column and the `parents` text[] documentation example hash lengths accordingly.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_git/git_commit.md` at line 16, Change the `hash` column\ndefinition from `varchar(40)` to `varchar(64)` (or `char(64)` if fixed-length is\npreferred) to support SHA-256 commit IDs; update any documentation examples that\nshow parent commit hashes (e.g., the `parents` text[] example) so their example\nhashes are 64 characters as well, and ensure any schema comments or constraints\nreferencing 40-char length are adjusted accordingly.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_ms365/ms365_teams_activity.md-255-257 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`255-257`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**`meetingCount` is absent from the Meeting Breakdown note — its relationship to the six sub-components is undefined**\n\nThe schema defines `meetingCount` as \"Total meetings count\" (Line 21), but the Meeting Breakdown note enumerates six sub-components (ad-hoc attended/organized, scheduled one-time attended/organized, scheduled recurring attended/organized) without ever referencing `meetingCount`. It is unclear whether:\n\n- `meetingCount` = the sum of all six components, or\n- `meetingCount` is a separate count from the Graph API that may not equal that sum (e.g., a meeting can be both attended and organized by the same user).\n\nClarify the relationship explicitly to prevent misuse in aggregation queries.\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Suggested addition to the Meeting Breakdown note</summary>\n\n```diff\n Total meetings = ad-hoc attended + ad-hoc organized + scheduled one-time attended + scheduled one-time organized + scheduled recurring attended + scheduled recurring organized. Use this breakdown to understand meeting culture.\n+\n+Note: `meetingCount` is the raw total reported by the Microsoft Graph API and may differ from the manual sum of the six sub-components above if a user both attended and organized the same meeting (double-counting). Prefer `meetingCount` for overall totals and the sub-components for behavioral breakdown.\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_ms365/ms365_teams_activity.md` around lines 255 - 257, Update the\n\"Meeting Breakdown\" note to explicitly define the relationship between\nmeetingCount and the six sub-components: state whether meetingCount is\ncalculated as the sum of ad-hoc attended, ad-hoc organized, scheduled one-time\nattended, scheduled one-time organized, scheduled recurring attended, and\nscheduled recurring organized, or whether it is a separate Graph API-provided\nvalue that may differ (e.g., because a single meeting can be both organized and\nattended by the same user). Mention the potential double-counting scenario and\nthe recommended aggregation approach (either use meetingCount directly if it is\nauthoritative, or sum the six components if you need component-level totals),\nreferencing meetingCount and the six component names so readers can find the\ncorresponding schema fields.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_ms365/ms365_sharepoint_activity.md-115-127 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`115-127`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**JOIN query can produce a cross-product if multiple `reportPeriod` rows exist per user/date, and is missing `od.isDeleted` filter**\n\nTwo issues in this example query:\n\n1. **Cross-product risk**: The schema stores rows with a composite of `(userPrincipalName, reportRefreshDate, reportPeriod)`. If both the SharePoint and OneDrive tables contain rows for the same user and `reportRefreshDate` but different `reportPeriod` values (e.g., \"7\" and \"30\" day reports ingested simultaneously), the JOIN on `userPrincipalName + reportRefreshDate` alone will produce a Cartesian product — multiplying rows per user. Add a `reportPeriod` equality condition to the join, or filter both sides to a single period first.\n\n2. **Missing deleted-user guard on the OneDrive side**: Only `sp.isDeleted = false` is filtered; `od.isDeleted` is unconstrained, so the join can include OneDrive rows for deleted users.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🛠️ Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n FROM ms365_sharepoint_activity sp\n JOIN ms365_onedrive_activity od ON sp.userPrincipalName = od.userPrincipalName\n   AND sp.reportRefreshDate = od.reportRefreshDate\n+  AND sp.reportPeriod = od.reportPeriod\n WHERE sp.reportRefreshDate = (SELECT MAX(reportRefreshDate) FROM ms365_sharepoint_activity)\n   AND sp.isDeleted = false\n+  AND od.isDeleted = false\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_ms365/ms365_sharepoint_activity.md` around lines 115 - 127, The\nJOIN risks producing a Cartesian product because rows are keyed by\n(userPrincipalName, reportRefreshDate, reportPeriod) and the query only joins on\nuserPrincipalName + reportRefreshDate; update the JOIN between\nms365_sharepoint_activity (sp) and ms365_onedrive_activity (od) to also require\nsp.reportPeriod = od.reportPeriod (or alternatively pre-filter both sp and od to\na single reportPeriod value), and also add od.isDeleted = false to the WHERE\nclause so deleted OneDrive rows are excluded; reference the symbols sp, od,\nreportPeriod, reportRefreshDate, userPrincipalName, and isDeleted when making\nthe change.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_ms365/ms365_onedrive_activity.md-20-20 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`20-20`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Change `lastActivityDate` to NULLABLE in both schema and Field Semantics to match MS Graph API behavior**\n\nThe sibling `ms365_email_activity.md` correctly marks `lastActivityDate` as `NULLABLE` with the note \"NULL if no activity in reporting period.\" The MS Graph API OneDrive activity report returns blank/empty values when a user has no activity during the reporting period, matching the behavior documented in the email activity schema. The current `NOT NULL` constraint on the OneDrive table will violate itself when Airbyte ingests rows with no activity.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-| `lastActivityDate` | date | NOT NULL | Last OneDrive activity date |\n+| `lastActivityDate` | date | NULLABLE | Last OneDrive activity date |\n```\n\nAnd update Field Semantics:\n\n```diff\n **`lastActivityDate`** (date, NOT NULL)\n-- **Purpose**: Date of last OneDrive activity\n+**`lastActivityDate`** (date, NULLABLE)\n+- **Purpose**: Date of last OneDrive activity\n+- **Note**: NULL if no activity in reporting period\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_ms365/ms365_onedrive_activity.md` at line 20, Update the OneDrive\nactivity schema to make the lastActivityDate column NULLABLE (change its\nconstraint from NOT NULL to NULLABLE) and update the Field Semantics for\nlastActivityDate to match ms365_email_activity.md by stating \"NULL if no\nactivity in reporting period\" (or equivalent phrasing); locate the symbol\nlastActivityDate in ms365_onedrive_activity.md and mirror the\nms365_email_activity.md wording so Airbyte can accept blank/empty OneDrive\nreport values without constraint violations.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_git/git_branch.md-19-19 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`19-19`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**`varchar(40)` accommodates SHA-1 only — consider `varchar(64)` for future SHA-256 compatibility.**\n\nGit 2.29+ introduced SHA-256 object format (64-hex characters). If the tracked repositories ever migrate, this column would silently truncate hashes. `varchar(64)` is the safe choice now with no storage cost.\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-| `last_commit_hash` | varchar(40) | NULLABLE | Last known commit SHA-1 hash on this branch |\n+| `last_commit_hash` | varchar(64) | NULLABLE | Last known commit hash on this branch (SHA-1: 40 chars, SHA-256: 64 chars) |\n```\n\nAnd in Field Semantics (line 57):\n```diff\n-**`last_commit_hash`** (varchar(40), NULLABLE)\n-- **Purpose**: Last known commit SHA on this branch\n-- **Format**: 40-character git SHA-1 hash\n+**`last_commit_hash`** (varchar(64), NULLABLE)\n+- **Purpose**: Last known commit SHA on this branch\n+- **Format**: 40-character SHA-1 or 64-character SHA-256 hash\n```\n</details>\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 57-57\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_git/git_branch.md` at line 19, Update the `last_commit_hash`\ncolumn definition to use `varchar(64)` instead of `varchar(40)` to support\nSHA-256 hashes, and update the corresponding Field Semantics description (the\nsection referencing `last_commit_hash` around line 57) to reflect that it may\ncontain either SHA-1 (40 hex) or SHA-256 (64 hex) commit hashes; search for the\nsymbol `last_commit_hash` in git_branch.md to find and adjust both occurrences.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_git/git_repo.md-74-77 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`74-77`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**`git.commit` child relationship is missing `On Delete` behavior — inconsistent with all other children.**\n\n`git.branch`, `git.file`, and `git.loc` all explicitly state `On Delete: CASCADE`. The omission for `git.commit` is ambiguous: does deleting a repo cascade-delete its commits, or are commits intentionally kept (RESTRICT / no action)? This should be explicitly documented either way.\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Proposed fix (if CASCADE is intended)</summary>\n\n```diff\n **`git.commit`**\n - **Join**: `gitlab_repo_id` → `repo_id`\n - **Cardinality**: One repository to many commits\n - **Description**: All commits belong to a repository\n+- **On Delete**: CASCADE\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_git/git_repo.md` around lines 74 - 77, The git.commit child\nrelationship currently omits the \"On Delete\" behavior, creating ambiguity;\nupdate the git.commit entry to explicitly state the intended cascade policy\n(e.g., add \"On Delete: CASCADE\" if commits should be removed when their repo is\ndeleted, or \"On Delete: RESTRICT/NO ACTION\" if they must be preserved) to match\nthe explicit statements present for git.branch, git.file, and git.loc; modify\nthe git.commit section in the schema documentation so it includes the chosen On\nDelete clause and a one-line justification matching the other child entries.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_youtrack/youtrack_issue.md-18-19 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`18-19`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**`timestamp` (no timezone) is inconsistent with `youtrack_issue_history.created_at` which uses `timestamptz`.**\n\nBoth columns originate from the same YouTrack API and represent moments in time. Mixing `timestamp` and `timestamptz` across related tables means cross-table comparisons (e.g., correlating an issue's `updated` with its history `created_at`) require explicit casts and are sensitive to the session `TimeZone` setting. Use `timestamptz` consistently.\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-| `created` | timestamp | NOT NULL | Issue creation timestamp |\n-| `updated` | timestamp | NOT NULL | Last update timestamp |\n+| `created` | timestamptz | NOT NULL | Issue creation timestamp |\n+| `updated` | timestamptz | NOT NULL | Last update timestamp |\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_youtrack/youtrack_issue.md` around lines 18 - 19, The issue: the\n`created` and `updated` columns are defined as type `timestamp` (no timezone)\nwhile related column `youtrack_issue_history.created_at` uses `timestamptz`,\ncausing inconsistent cross-table time comparisons; fix by altering the schema\ndefinitions for the `created` and `updated` columns to use `timestamptz` instead\nof `timestamp` so all timestamps from the YouTrack API are stored as\ntimezone-aware values, and update any DDL/migration that defines\n`created`/`updated` (and any associated INSERT/UPDATE conversion logic) to\nexplicitly use timestamptz to keep comparisons with\n`youtrack_issue_history.created_at` consistent.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_youtrack/youtrack_issue_history.md-135-151 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`135-151`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**`value::text` on a JSONB string produces JSON-encoded output with outer double quotes, not bare text.**\n\nIn PostgreSQL, `jsonb::text` produces the JSON text representation. For a state stored as the JSONB string `\"In Progress\"`, `value::text` yields `\"In Progress\"` (with surrounding double quotes as part of the text), making the `state` column unsuitable for display or string comparison. Use `value #>> '{}'` to extract the bare scalar text.\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n WITH state_changes AS (\n     SELECT\n         id_readable,\n-        value::text as state,\n+        value #>> '{}' as state,\n         created_at,\n         LEAD(created_at) OVER (PARTITION BY id_readable ORDER BY created_at) as next_change\n     FROM youtrack_issue_history\n     WHERE field_name = 'State'\n )\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_youtrack/youtrack_issue_history.md` around lines 135 - 151, The\nstate column is being set via value::text which yields a JSON-encoded string\nwith surrounding quotes; update the CTE/select to extract the bare scalar text\nby replacing the cast with the jsonb text-extraction operator (use value #>>\n'{}') so that the state in state_changes (and final SELECT) contains unquoted\ntext for display and comparisons, keeping the rest of the logic (CTE name\nstate_changes, LEAD(created_at) partitioning, hours_in_state computation)\nintact.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (16)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_zulip/zulip_users.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`23-25`: **`uuid` and `email` indexes should be UNIQUE**\n\nBoth fields are described as unique identifiers — `uuid` as a \"Universally unique identifier\" and `email` as the \"primary key for correlating Zulip users with identities in other systems.\" Plain (non-unique) indexes don't enforce that uniqueness at the database level. If uniqueness is guaranteed upstream by Zulip/Airbyte, that assumption should be documented explicitly; otherwise these should be UNIQUE indexes.\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-**Indexes**:\n-- `idx_zulip_users_email`: `(email)`\n-- `idx_zulip_users_uuid`: `(uuid)`\n+**Indexes**:\n+- `idx_zulip_users_email`: `(email)` UNIQUE\n+- `idx_zulip_users_uuid`: `(uuid)` UNIQUE\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_zulip/zulip_users.md` around lines 23 - 25, The schema declares\nindexes idx_zulip_users_email and idx_zulip_users_uuid on email and uuid but\nthose fields are described as unique identifiers, so change these to UNIQUE\nindexes (or add UNIQUE constraints) to enforce uniqueness at the DB level;\nupdate the index definitions for email and uuid (idx_zulip_users_email,\nidx_zulip_users_uuid) to be UNIQUE, or add explicit UNIQUE constraints on the\nemail and uuid columns, and if uniqueness is guaranteed upstream instead, add\ndocumentation stating that assumption and why UNIQUE constraints are omitted.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`16-16`: **`role` column typed as `numeric` instead of an integer type**\n\nThe documented role codes (100, 200, 300, 400, 600) are whole numbers. Using `numeric` (arbitrary-precision decimal) for what is effectively a small fixed-set integer wastes space and is semantically misleading. `integer` or `smallint` would be appropriate.\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-| `role` | numeric | NOT NULL | User role level |\n+| `role` | smallint | NOT NULL | User role level |\n```\n\n```diff\n-**`role`** (numeric, NOT NULL)\n+**`role`** (smallint, NOT NULL)\n```\n</details>\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 60-60\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_zulip/zulip_users.md` at line 16, The `role` column is declared\nas `numeric` but represents small fixed integer codes (100, 200, 300, 400, 600);\nupdate the column type from `numeric` to an integer type (preferably `smallint`\nor `integer`) in the schema/documentation where `role` is defined (refer to the\n`role` column entry and the repeated occurrence noted as \"Also applies to:\n60-60\") so the type correctly reflects whole-number role codes and avoids\narbitrary-precision decimals.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_zulip/zulip_messages.md (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`17-17`: **Document the FK constraint for `sender_id`**\n\nThe Field Semantics and Relationships sections both acknowledge `sender_id` references `zulip_users.id`, but the Schema Definition table has no FK constraint entry. If the constraint exists in the DDL, add it here for completeness; if it's intentionally omitted (e.g., for Airbyte load performance), document that rationale in the Notes section.\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix (if constraint exists)</summary>\n\n```diff\n | `sender_id` | bigint | NOT NULL | Sender's Zulip user ID |\n+| `sender_id` | bigint | NOT NULL, FK → zulip_users(id) | Sender's Zulip user ID |\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_zulip/zulip_messages.md` at line 17, The Schema Definition table\nis missing the FK constraint for sender_id even though Field Semantics and\nRelationships state it references zulip_users.id; either add an explicit FK\nentry for sender_id -> zulip_users.id in the Schema Definition table (include\nconstraint name if present in DDL) or, if the constraint is intentionally\nomitted (e.g., for Airbyte performance), update the Notes section to state that\nrationale and reference the DDL behavior; update the entry for sender_id and the\nNotes accordingly to keep schema docs consistent with the DDL.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`57-63`: **Inconsistent relationship arrow direction vs `zulip_users.md`**\n\n`zulip_users.md` (line 77) writes the join as `id → sender_id` (parent → child / left-to-right), while this file writes it as `sender_id ← id` (child ← parent / right-to-left). They express the same relationship but use opposing conventions. Standardise on one direction across all stream docs.\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix (matching zulip_users.md convention)</summary>\n\n```diff\n-**Join**: `sender_id` ← `id`\n+**Join**: `id` → `sender_id`\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_zulip/zulip_messages.md` around lines 57 - 63, The Parent section\nin zulip_messages (the Join line currently reading \"sender_id ← id\") uses the\nopposite arrow convention to zulip_users (which uses \"id → sender_id\"); change\nthe Join arrow in the Parent block of zulip_messages to \"id → sender_id\" so it\nmatches the id → sender_id (parent → child / left-to-right) convention used in\nzulip_users.md and standardise arrow direction across stream docs.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`16-16`: **`count` column: naming conflict with SQL aggregate + wrong data type**\n\nTwo distinct issues:\n\n1. **Naming**: Using `count` as a column name shadows the built-in SQL aggregate `COUNT()`. The \"Daily message volume\" example at line 89 already does `SUM(count) as messages` alongside `COUNT(DISTINCT sender_id)` in the same query — this is a persistent readability hazard for every consumer of this table. Prefer `message_count` or `msg_count`.\n\n2. **Type**: The Field Semantics section describes the value as \"Positive integer,\" but the schema declares it `numeric`. Use `integer` or `bigint` to match the stated semantics.\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-| `count` | numeric | NOT NULL | Message count |\n+| `message_count` | integer | NOT NULL | Message count |\n```\n\n```diff\n-**`count`** (numeric, NOT NULL)\n+**`message_count`** (integer, NOT NULL)\n - **Purpose**: Number of messages in this record\n - **Format**: Positive integer\n```\n</details>\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 41-44\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_zulip/zulip_messages.md` at line 16, Rename the `count` column to\na non-ambiguous name (e.g., `message_count` or `msg_count`) and change its SQL\ntype from `numeric` to an integer type matching semantics (use `integer` or\n`bigint` for \"Positive integer\"); update all references to `count` in this\ndocument (including the \"Daily message volume\" example that uses `SUM(count) as\nmessages` and any Field Semantics text) so they use the new column name and the\nnew type consistently, and ensure any example queries and descriptions reflect\n`message_count`/`msg_count` and the integer type.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_cursor/cursor_daily_usage.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`13-42`: **26-column wide table — consider documenting the design rationale**\n\nTables with more than 15 columns are regarded as wide tables and should be considered for vertical splitting. Because of the MVCC mechanism, the write amplification of wide tables is more obvious, so try to reduce frequent updates. If this table is written to daily per user, the high column count will amplify write overhead. A short design note explaining why denormalization is preferred here (e.g., query simplicity, Airbyte schema fidelity) would aid future contributors considering splits or partial updates.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_cursor/cursor_daily_usage.md` around lines 13 - 42, The schema\ndoc for cursor_daily_usage lists 26 columns (e.g., unique, day, date, email,\nuserId, apiKeyReqs, cmdkUsages, etc.) but lacks a design rationale for the wide,\ndenormalized layout; add a short \"Design Rationale\" paragraph below the table\nthat explains why denormalization was chosen (query simplicity for\ndaily-per-user reads, compatibility with Airbyte ingestion/schema fidelity,\nreduced read-side join cost), call out MVCC/write-amplification tradeoffs and\nthe expected write pattern (batched/daily updates per user) that mitigates\nfrequent small updates, and note alternatives considered (vertical\nsplit/normalized tables) and why they were rejected to guide future\ncontributors.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_git/git_num_stat.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`15-15`: **`id integer` risks overflow for large multi-repo deployments.**\n\nEach `git.num_stat` row represents one file's changes in one commit. At scale — many repos, each with many commits and files per commit — the total row count can easily exceed the ~2.1 billion `integer` ceiling. Since `commit_id` is already declared as `bigint`, using `integer` here is inconsistent. Suggest promoting `id` to `bigint`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Suggested schema change</summary>\n\n```diff\n-| `id` | integer | PRIMARY KEY, AUTO INCREMENT | Internal primary key |\n+| `id` | bigint  | PRIMARY KEY, AUTO INCREMENT | Internal primary key |\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_git/git_num_stat.md` at line 15, The primary key column `id` in\ntable `git.num_stat` is declared as `integer` and risks overflow; change `id` to\na 64-bit type to match `commit_id` and support large multi-repo deployments.\nLocate the `git.num_stat` table definition and replace the `id integer PRIMARY\nKEY, AUTO INCREMENT` declaration with a bigint-based auto-increment (e.g.,\n`bigint`/`bigserial` or an equivalent BIGINT with a sequence) so the PK is\n64-bit; update any sequence names, constraints, and references to `id` (indexes,\nFK relations, code that assumes integer) accordingly. Ensure migrations alter\nthe column type safely (create new bigint sequence if needed, backfill/cast\nexisting values, swap constraints) and update any schema docs mentioning `id`.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/stream_communication/communication_events.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`27-27`: **`count` should be `integer` or `bigint`, not `numeric`.**\n\n`numeric` is arbitrary-precision decimal storage — appropriate for monetary or fractional values, but unnecessary overhead for an activity event count that is always a non-negative whole number. Use `bigint` to match the scale of potential row counts and reduce storage overhead.\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Suggested type change</summary>\n\n```diff\n-| `count` | numeric | NOT NULL | Number of events/messages |\n+| `count` | bigint  | NOT NULL | Number of events/messages |\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 131-134\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/stream_communication/communication_events.md` at line 27, The `count`\ncolumn in the communication_events schema is declared as `numeric` but should be\nan integer type; update all occurrences of the `count` column (e.g., the table\nrow showing \"`count` | numeric | NOT NULL | Number of events/messages\" and the\nother instance referenced at lines ~131-134) to use `bigint` (or `integer` if\nyou expect small values) instead of `numeric` so the docs/schema reflect a\nnon-negative whole-number event count and avoid unnecessary decimal storage.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`39-39`: **`(source, source_id)` UNIQUE is redundant — `source_id` already embeds the source.**\n\n`source_id` is documented as `{source}:{unique_id}:{channel}` (MS365) or `{source}:{unique_id}` (Zulip), so it already encodes the source platform. The composite UNIQUE on `(source, source_id)` carries the same deduplication guarantee as a simple UNIQUE on `(source_id)`, at the cost of a wider index. A UNIQUE constraint on `source_id` alone suffices and is consistent with the `idx_comm_events_source_id` single-column index already defined.\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Suggested simplification</summary>\n\n```diff\n-- `(source, source_id)` — UNIQUE, prevents duplicate ingestion from same source record\n+- `(source_id)` — UNIQUE, prevents duplicate ingestion from same source record\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 57-65\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/stream_communication/communication_events.md` at line 39, The\ncomposite UNIQUE constraint on (source, source_id) is redundant because\nsource_id already encodes the source; remove the composite UNIQUE and replace it\nwith (or ensure use of) a single-column UNIQUE on source_id instead, and update\nany related references to use the existing idx_comm_events_source_id\nsingle-column index; specifically change the constraint definition mentioning\n(source, source_id) in communication_events.md to just UNIQUE(source_id) and\nremove duplicate mentions in the other occurrences (lines 57–65) so the docs and\nconstraints align with idx_comm_events_source_id.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_git/git_commit.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`22-22`: **`default_branch` boolean flag has no documented staleness/update strategy.**\n\nThis flag is a mutable denormalized attribute: a commit that starts on a feature branch can later appear on the default branch after a merge or squash. When that happens, every affected historical record must be retroactively flipped. Likewise, if a repository's default branch is renamed or changed, the entire commit table for that repo may need a bulk update.\n\nNeither of these scenarios is documented in the Notes section, leaving implementers without guidance on when/how to keep the flag current. Consider either:\n- Documenting the refresh cadence and trigger (e.g., \"updated nightly by re-querying the default branch commit list\"), or\n- Modeling branch membership in a separate `git_commit_branch` junction table to avoid mutable flags on immutable commit records.\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 84-88, 195-197\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_git/git_commit.md` at line 22, The `default_branch` boolean on\ngit_commit is a mutable denormalized attribute without guidance; update the\nNotes for `git_commit` to explain staleness and a refresh/update strategy (e.g.,\nnightly reconciliation that re-queries the repo's default branch commits and\nbulk-updates affected `git_commit.default_branch` values, plus triggers on\nmerges/branch-rename events), and/or document an alternative schema approach\nusing a `git_commit_branch` junction table to model branch membership instead of\nmutating commits; reference the `default_branch` flag and the `git_commit`\nrecord in the Notes and add recommended cadence and triggers (nightly job,\nwebhooks on merge/rename, bulk-update procedure) so implementers know how to\nkeep the flag current.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_git/git_file.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`112-114`: **Consider adding a deletion-status field to the schema.**\n\nThe Notes section states this table stores all files ever, including deleted ones, but the schema has no column to distinguish a currently-present file from a deleted one. Consumers are forced to infer this from the absence of recent `git.loc` or `git.num_stat` records, which is fragile and requires joins.\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Suggested addition to schema</summary>\n\n```diff\n | `created_at` | timestamp | DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP | Record creation timestamp |\n+| `deleted_at` | timestamp | NULLABLE | When the file was last observed missing; NULL if currently present |\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_git/git_file.md` around lines 112 - 114, The git_file historical\ntable lacks an explicit deletion-status column, forcing consumers to infer\ndeletions from absent git.loc or git.num_stat rows; add a nullable boolean\ncolumn like is_deleted (or status ENUM) and an optional deleted_at timestamp to\nthe git_file schema, update any ingest/migration logic that writes to git_file\n(and references git.loc/git.num_stat) to set is_deleted=true and deleted_at when\na file deletion is observed, and update the documentation in git_file.md to\ndescribe the new columns and their semantics so downstream joins and queries no\nlonger need fragile heuristics to detect deletions.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_ms365/ms365_onedrive_activity.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`22-22`: **`userPrincipalName` is PII (email address) — add a data classification/privacy note**\n\n`userPrincipalName` is documented as an email-format identifier and is present as a queryable, indexed field across all four MS365 tables. None of the docs include any data sensitivity classification, retention policy guidance, or GDPR/CCPA notes for this field.\n\nConsider adding a **Data Privacy** section noting:\n- This field constitutes PII (user email).\n- Access should be restricted to authorized roles.\n- Retention policies should align with applicable regulations (e.g., GDPR right to erasure for `isDeleted = true` records).\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_ms365/ms365_onedrive_activity.md` at line 22, Add a \"Data\nPrivacy\" section to the MS365 OneDrive activity docs that explicitly classifies\nthe userPrincipalName field as PII (email address), states it appears across the\nMS365 tables (userPrincipalName), and prescribes access controls (restrict to\nauthorized roles), retention guidance (align with retention policies and\nconsider removal/archival when isDeleted = true), and GDPR/CCPA notes (support\nright-to-erasure and other regulatory requirements). Make the guidance concise\nand include examples for enforcement points: role-based access, query/index\nrestrictions, and retention/erasure handling for isDeleted records.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_ms365/ms365_sharepoint_activity.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`148-148`: **Three consecutive sentences open with \"SharePoint\" — consider rewording for readability**\n\nAs flagged by LanguageTool, the paragraph has three successive sentences starting with the same word, which reduces readability.\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Suggested rewrite</summary>\n\n```diff\n-SharePoint and OneDrive share similar metrics (file views, sync, sharing) but track different scopes. SharePoint covers team sites and document libraries, while OneDrive tracks personal file storage. The `visitedPageCount` metric is unique to SharePoint.\n+SharePoint and OneDrive share similar metrics (file views, sync, sharing) but track different scopes: team sites and document libraries for SharePoint versus personal file storage for OneDrive. The `visitedPageCount` metric is unique to SharePoint.\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_ms365/ms365_sharepoint_activity.md` at line 148, The three\nconsecutive sentences starting with \"SharePoint\" reduce readability; reword the\nparagraph to vary sentence openings and improve flow by, for example, starting\none sentence with \"OneDrive\" or a transitional phrase and by combining related\nclauses: keep the facts that SharePoint covers team sites and document\nlibraries, OneDrive tracks personal file storage, and that the visitedPageCount\nmetric is unique to SharePoint (reference: visitedPageCount) while ensuring no\nmore than one sentence begins with \"SharePoint.\"\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_youtrack/youtrack_user.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`14-19`: **`youtrack_user` is the only table in this PR without a `created_at` audit field.**\n\nEvery other table (`git.author`, `git.repo`, `git.branch`, `git.loc`, `youtrack_issue`, `youtrack_issue_history`) includes a `created_at` timestamp for audit and data-lineage purposes. Adding one here keeps the schema consistent and supports incremental sync detection.\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Proposed addition</summary>\n\n```diff\n | `username`     | varchar | NOT NULL, UNIQUE   | YouTrack username        |\n+| `created_at`   | timestamptz | DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP | Record creation timestamp |\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_youtrack/youtrack_user.md` around lines 14 - 19, The\nyoutrack_user table is missing a created_at audit timestamp; add a NOT NULL\ncreated_at column (timestamp/datetime) with a sensible default (e.g.,\nCURRENT_TIMESTAMP) to the youtrack_user table definition and any corresponding\nmigration (and ensure any ORM model/DDL for youtrack_user includes created_at)\nso the schema matches other tables (youtrack_issue, youtrack_issue_history,\ngit.author, etc.) and supports incremental sync detection.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_youtrack/youtrack_issue.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`22-23`: **Explicit indexes on `id_readable` and `youtrack_id` are redundant — UNIQUE constraints already create B-tree indexes in PostgreSQL.**\n\nDocumenting them as separate index objects implies they exist as additional structures, which could mislead DDL writers into creating duplicate indexes. Remove them from the Indexes section or note that these are the indexes backing the unique constraints.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_youtrack/youtrack_issue.md` around lines 22 - 23, The Indexes\nsection incorrectly lists explicit indexes youtrack_issue_idx_id_readable and\nyoutrack_issue_idx_youtrack_id which are redundant because the UNIQUE\nconstraints on id_readable and youtrack_id already create B-tree indexes; remove\nthose two index entries from the Indexes section (or replace them with a single\nnote stating that the UNIQUE constraints on id_readable and youtrack_id\nimplicitly create the backing indexes) so readers aren’t misled into creating\nduplicate indexes.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>streams/raw_git/git_branch.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`26-26`: **Boolean index on `is_default` has near-zero selectivity — prefer a partial index.**\n\nVirtually every row has `is_default = false`; a full index on this column adds write overhead with minimal query benefit. A partial index `WHERE is_default = true` is smaller, faster to scan, and directly serves the \"list default branches\" query pattern.\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-- `is_default` — default branch queries\n+- `is_default` (PARTIAL, WHERE is_default = true) — default branch queries\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/raw_git/git_branch.md` at line 26, The boolean column `is_default`\nhas near-zero selectivity so replace the existing full index with a partial\nindex that only covers rows WHERE is_default = true to avoid write overhead: add\na migration that creates the partial index (e.g., CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY ...\nON <table> (is_default) WHERE is_default = true), drop the old full index\nCONCURRENTLY, and ensure the migration and any schema docs reference\n`is_default` and the new index name; run this as a safe, concurrent migration so\nreads still work while switching indexes.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>ℹ️ Review info</summary>\n\n**Configuration used**: defaults\n\n**Review profile**: CHILL\n\n**Plan**: Pro\n\n<details>\n<summary>📥 Commits</summary>\n\nReviewing files that changed from the base of the PR and between 58f37b6c0eb3500fbb3104cee91099c34d3045b4 and 9e1edb2dcc6d5ea476ba219a7577b99ed3772afd.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>📒 Files selected for processing (21)</summary>\n\n* `streams/raw_cursor/cursor_daily_usage.md`\n* `streams/raw_cursor/cursor_events.md`\n* `streams/raw_cursor/cursor_events_token_usage.md`\n* `streams/raw_git/git_author.md`\n* `streams/raw_git/git_branch.md`\n* `streams/raw_git/git_commit.md`\n* `streams/raw_git/git_file.md`\n* `streams/raw_git/git_loc.md`\n* `streams/raw_git/git_num_stat.md`\n* `streams/raw_git/git_repo.md`\n* `streams/raw_ms365/ms365_email_activity.md`\n* `streams/raw_ms365/ms365_onedrive_activity.md`\n* `streams/raw_ms365/ms365_sharepoint_activity.md`\n* `streams/raw_ms365/ms365_teams_activity.md`\n* `streams/raw_youtrack/youtrack_issue.md`\n* `streams/raw_youtrack/youtrack_issue_history.md`\n* `streams/raw_youtrack/youtrack_user.md`\n* `streams/raw_zulip/zulip_messages.md`\n* `streams/raw_zulip/zulip_users.md`\n* `streams/stream_communication/communication_events.md`\n* `streams/stream_task_tracker/task_tracker.md`\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/3#pullrequestreview-3840072782","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/3","author_association":"NONE","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/3#pullrequestreview-3840072782"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/3"}},"submitted_at":"2026-02-23T09:54:27Z","commit_id":"9e1edb2dcc6d5ea476ba219a7577b99ed3772afd"},{"id":3840288777,"node_id":"PRR_kwDORRWv8s7k5igJ","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 4**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>streams/stream_task_tracker/task.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`17-18`: **Consider `timestamptz` instead of `timestamp` for all temporal columns.**\n\nPostgreSQL `timestamp` is timezone-naive. With data ingested from multiple sources (YouTrack, Jira, and potentially others), each potentially emitting timestamps in different local timezones, all interval metrics (cycle time, lead time, throughput) and cross-stream correlations will silently compute wrong durations if sources use different offsets.\n\n`timestamptz` stores a UTC epoch and renders it per session timezone, making cross-source comparisons safe.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Proposed change (applies to all timestamp columns)</summary>\n\n```diff\n-| `ingestion_date` | timestamp  | NOT NULL | When this record was ingested |\n+| `ingestion_date` | timestamptz | NOT NULL | When this record was ingested |\n ...\n-| `created_at`    | timestamp  | NOT NULL | Task creation datetime |\n+| `created_at`    | timestamptz | NOT NULL | Task creation datetime |\n-| `started_at`    | timestamp  | NULLABLE | When work on the task began |\n+| `started_at`    | timestamptz | NULLABLE | When work on the task began |\n-| `to_verify_at`  | timestamp  | NULLABLE | When task was moved to verification |\n+| `to_verify_at`  | timestamptz | NULLABLE | When task was moved to verification |\n-| `done_at`       | timestamp  | NULLABLE | Task completion datetime |\n+| `done_at`       | timestamptz | NULLABLE | Task completion datetime |\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 32-43\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/stream_task_tracker/task.md` around lines 17 - 18, Change all\ntemporal columns from PostgreSQL's timezone-naive type to timestamptz: replace\n`timestamp` with `timestamptz` for `ingestion_date` and every other temporal\ncolumn in this schema (see the other timestamp columns referenced around lines\n32-43) so stored times are UTC-aware; update any column definitions and any\nDDL/CREATE statements that use `timestamp` in this file\n(`stream_task_tracker/task.md`) to `timestamptz`, and ensure any related\ndocumentation/comments reflect the timezone-aware type.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`55-55`: **UNIQUE constraint on `(source, source_id)` is unnamed, inconsistent with all other indexes.**\n\nEvery other index follows the `idx_task_*` naming convention. The unnamed unique constraint makes it harder to reference in error messages, migrations, or `DROP INDEX`.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-- `(source, source_id)` — UNIQUE, prevents duplicates\n+- `uq_task_source_source_id`: `(source, source_id)` — UNIQUE, prevents duplicates\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@streams/stream_task_tracker/task.md` at line 55, The unnamed UNIQUE\nconstraint on (source, source_id) should be given a consistent name following\nthe idx_task_* convention; update the schema/migration that defines the UNIQUE\non (source, source_id) to use a descriptive index name (e.g.,\nidx_task_source_source_id or similar) so it matches other indices and can be\nreferenced in errors/migrations/DDL; locate the constraint definition that\ncreates the UNIQUE for (source, source_id) and replace the anonymous constraint\nwith the named constraint while ensuring any tests or DROP INDEX/migration steps\nuse the new name.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for all review comments with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nInline comments:\nIn `@streams/stream_task_tracker/task.md`:\n- Line 22: The join using task.user_story_id = user_story.task_id must include a\nsource guard to avoid cross-source collisions: update the relationship\ndescription that references user_story_id and any examples (including the note)\nto require AND t.source = us.source (or equivalent aliases) when joining task ->\nuser_story; update all example queries and the relationship/note text to show\nthe safe pattern (e.g., JOIN user_story us ON t.user_story_id = us.task_id AND\nt.source = us.source) and mention that source must match because IDs are not\nglobally unique across YouTrack and Jira.\n- Line 192: The doc line stating \"Correlate task activity with\n`stream_communication` events via assignee email\" is inconsistent with the\nschema (which only has `assignee_id` and `assignee_name`); update the\ndocumentation for `stream_task_tracker` to either add an `assignee_email` column\nto the schema, or explicitly state that `assignee_id` will contain the user's\nemail for trackers that support it (e.g., Jira), or point to the\nidentity-resolution mapping table that joins `assignee_id` to emails so\nconsumers know how to perform the correlation with `stream_communication`;\nreference `assignee_id`, `assignee_name`, and `stream_communication` in the\nchange so it's clear where to apply the fix.\n- Around line 296-298: The not_started bucket currently counts rows with\nstarted_at IS NULL even if done_at IS NOT NULL, double-counting tasks that\nskipped straight to done; modify the not_started FILTER to require both\nstarted_at IS NULL AND done_at IS NULL (i.e., COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE started_at\nIS NULL AND done_at IS NULL) AS not_started) so skipped-to-done tasks remain\nonly in the completed bucket and the three buckets sum to total_tasks; update\nany dependent metrics (e.g., completion_pct) if they assume the old semantics.\n- Around line 260-266: The WHERE clause currently uses \"t.done_at >=\n'2026-01-01'\" which implicitly excludes NULL (in-progress) tasks; update the\nquery to make the intent explicit by adding \"AND t.done_at IS NOT NULL\" if you\nmean to only include completed tasks, or change the predicate (e.g., remove the\ndate comparison or add an OR condition) if you intend to include in-progress\ntasks with commits; reference the task alias/column (t.done_at) in the fix so\nthe filter is unambiguous.\n\n---\n\nNitpick comments:\nIn `@streams/stream_task_tracker/task.md`:\n- Around line 17-18: Change all temporal columns from PostgreSQL's\ntimezone-naive type to timestamptz: replace `timestamp` with `timestamptz` for\n`ingestion_date` and every other temporal column in this schema (see the other\ntimestamp columns referenced around lines 32-43) so stored times are UTC-aware;\nupdate any column definitions and any DDL/CREATE statements that use `timestamp`\nin this file (`stream_task_tracker/task.md`) to `timestamptz`, and ensure any\nrelated documentation/comments reflect the timezone-aware type.\n- Line 55: The unnamed UNIQUE constraint on (source, source_id) should be given\na consistent name following the idx_task_* convention; update the\nschema/migration that defines the UNIQUE on (source, source_id) to use a\ndescriptive index name (e.g., idx_task_source_source_id or similar) so it\nmatches other indices and can be referenced in errors/migrations/DDL; locate the\nconstraint definition that creates the UNIQUE for (source, source_id) and\nreplace the anonymous constraint with the named constraint while ensuring any\ntests or DROP INDEX/migration steps use the new name.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>ℹ️ Review info</summary>\n\n**Configuration used**: defaults\n\n**Review profile**: CHILL\n\n**Plan**: Pro\n\n<details>\n<summary>📥 Commits</summary>\n\nReviewing files that changed from the base of the PR and between 9e1edb2dcc6d5ea476ba219a7577b99ed3772afd and b079580cc3ce130cb78f98ecae10f237fb10b604.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>📒 Files selected for processing (3)</summary>\n\n* `streams/stream_task_tracker/epic.md`\n* `streams/stream_task_tracker/task.md`\n* `streams/stream_task_tracker/user_story.md`\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>✅ Files skipped from review due to trivial changes (2)</summary>\n\n* streams/stream_task_tracker/epic.md\n* streams/stream_task_tracker/user_story.md\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/3#pullrequestreview-3840288777","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/3","author_association":"NONE","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pull/3#pullrequestreview-3840288777"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-insight/pulls/3"}},"submitted_at":"2026-02-23T10:41:21Z","commit_id":"b079580cc3ce130cb78f98ecae10f237fb10b604"},{"id":3860587738,"node_id":"PRR_kwDORRWv8s7mG-Ta","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.gi